  "surprise": "Static two-stream mHC (one hyper-connection per Peri-LN block, closed-form 2x2 doubly stochastic mix) scored 624/593/625 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 136-fable's 655/673/646: mean 614 vs 658, -44, every seed lower. My throughput prediction was wrong: pretraining ran 2377/2363/2374 steps vs 136's 3370 (-30%, predicted -5 to -15%); the unfused fp32 elementwise stream ops and their scalar-weight gradient reductions cost far more than their FLOPs. Sampling was not slowed (256-351 samples/s, total 1500-1627 s). With 30% fewer steps the final val loss was LOWER (0.0816/0.0840/0.0829 vs 136 seed 0 0.0860) and held-out greedy similar (0.970/0.952/0.967), yet RL round 1 solved only 276/370/524 targets (136 seed 0: 577); RL caught up by round 4 (1104/1045/1091 vs 1102), but eval did not. Val loss again failed to predict the metric. The hyper-connections barely left their init, identically on all three seeds: reads 0.881/0.119 -> 0.81-0.86/0.08-0.10, mix p 0.881 -> 0.85-0.91, writes 0.79-1.13 (block 2 writes 0.80 to stream 0 vs 0.96 to stream 1). Interpretation, not tested: the scratch stream was used only weakly, so this run is mostly 136 with 30% less pretraining; mHC at this scale needs a fused or compiled implementation before its per-step effect can be judged at matched steps.",
